"""Supplementary narrative; numerical tables are generated from verified records."""
BLOCKS=[]
def S(identifier,kind,text='',**kwargs):BLOCKS.append(dict(id=identifier,kind=kind,en=text,**kwargs))

S('s1','h1','S1 Specimen records and evaluation populations')
S('s1a','p','The input version contains 18 workbooks and 1,672 physical specimen records. The time-domain, frequency-domain, absorption and refractive-index sheets are linked views, not replicate specimens. Labels distinguish Zaoren from Suanzaoren throughout the analysis. Frozen specimen roles total TRAIN999, CAL334 and PRED339. The five Suanzaoren source workbooks supply 300 TRAIN, 100 CAL and 100 PRED records. The paired optical modelling grid contains 612 coordinates within 0.1–1.5 THz. Source workbook identifiers, labels and original bytes are preserved.')
S('s1_table','table',key='workbooks')
S('s1b','p','The Zaoren workbooks contain 350, 5, 3, 3 and 3 specimens. Consequently, its fixed-role source task has 70 of 74 PRED specimens in the majority source. Accuracy, macro-F1 and balanced accuracy answer different questions in that task. Each Suanzaoren source is represented by one workbook; holding out that workbook also removes its source class. Closed-set source generalization to new batches therefore requires independently acquired examples of every source class in training and evaluation. The present LOWO analyses concern material classes, each represented by several workbooks.')

S('s2','h1','S2 Optical definitions and exported numerical scales')
S('s2a','p','The analysis uses the existing α and n exports, with no independent reconstruction of their software extraction. For physical notation, a conventional homogeneous-slab approximation relates a corrected effective equivalent-air-reference transfer function T to refractive index n and absorption coefficient α as follows. With unwrapped reference-minus-sample phase Φ, the corresponding index and absorption definitions are given in Eqs. (S1)–(S3). The source reference is Duvillaret, Garet and Coutaz, cited in the main manuscript.')
S('s2eq1','equation',key='optical_transfer')
S('s2eq2','equation',key='optical_index')
S('s2eq3','equation',key='optical_absorption')
S('s2b','p','Here ω = 2πf, d is thickness and c is the speed of light. These expressions assume an effective plane-parallel slab near normal incidence, with internal reflections neglected or handled separately. T in these definitions is not an uncorrected sample/polyethylene ratio. Consistent SI lengths would give α in m⁻¹, but this dimensional statement does not establish the numerical unit of the existing export. The manuscript therefore retains its original exported scale. Nominal header thicknesses are not substituted for measured values, and refractive-index values are not adjusted toward a presumed organic-material range. These equations define quantities; they do not certify an undocumented extraction implementation.')

S('s3','h1','S3 Estimator settings and a reusable selection interface')
S('s3a','p','The principal selection scheme uses per-spectrum SNV with sample standard deviation (ddof = 1), followed by training-fitted StandardScaler. Each class-stratified draw samples 80% of the current fitting class without replacement, rounding upward. Predictor columns and one-versus-rest responses are standardized with population standard deviations for the two-component PLS1 fits. The MC20 score combines absolute mean coefficients divided by their sample standard deviations using an across-class Euclidean norm. The denominator floor follows the main-text definition. Neither the outer labels nor the full collection determine ranks or scaler statistics.')
S('s3b','p','Eligible primary K values are 31, 62, 123, 184 and 245. RBF-SVM C values are 0.1, 1, 10 and 100, and kernel scale factors are 1, 0.1 and 10 in that tie order. Scale uses the actual selected fitting matrix after any block weighting. Joint SNV transforms n and α separately before concatenation. The raw-n/SNV-α weighted variant selects the n-block multiplier from 1, 0.5 and 0.25. Equal-block selection has separate rankings and therefore a distinct ranking cost. MC100 changes the number of coefficient draws; five-stream MC20 aggregates independently generated rankings. The unchanged original configuration tables retain all eligible alternatives.')
S('s3_interface','table',key='interface')
S('s3c','p','The reusable component is the training-only ranking and coordinate-projection interface. It accepts a labelled fitting matrix and returns scores and selected coordinates; the final decision rule is supplied separately. To use it with a different classifier, preprocessing, ranking, K and that classifier’s eligible settings must stay inside the appropriate validation loop. An SVM-selected K is not automatically optimal for LDA, an MLP or another estimator. This separation is an architectural capability of the workflow. The measured selection gain reported here uses SVM; the full-spectrum LDA and MLP rows do not constitute a cross-classifier test of selected features.')
S('s3_primary','table',key='all_primary')
S('s3d','p','Shrinkage LDA selects its eligible raw/SNV preprocessing and uses automatic shrinkage. The saved MLP reference selects raw/SNV preprocessing and 32/64 hidden units, with Adam, learning rate 0.001, L2 alpha 0.01, batch size 32, maximum 300 iterations, early stopping on 20% of the current fitting subset, patience 15 and random seed 42. Outer fitting therefore includes an early-stopping holdout of approximately 48 of 240 specimens; an inner 120-specimen fit includes approximately 24. Those holdouts do not receive gradient updates, although they remain inside the outer-training population.')
S('s3_nn','table',key='mlp_diagnostics')
S('s3e','p','The 15 saved MLP checkpoints reproduce their recorded outer predictions. They stop after 20–42 iterations, median 29, rather than at the maximum iteration count. Mean replay accuracy on the entire outer-fitting set is 93.06%, including the early-stopping holdout; mean outer-test accuracy is 73.22%. The replay is not a pure gradient-training score, and zero convergence warnings do not establish complete hyperparameter optimization. The selected-SVM comparison changes both features and estimator fitting; the same-input-width full-spectrum SVM supplies a more informative reference for the observed gap. The replay therefore describes the evaluated reference and its stopping behavior.')
S('s3_e1','table',key='e1_parameters')
S('s3f','p','The E1 full-spectrum bridge preserves all 15 original outer partitions and every original inner fitting and validation ID. All 360 inner fits reach the three distinct numerical gamma values, and the 15 outer refits complete the recorded 375 fits. Their parameters and pooled predictions are supplied in the data workbook. This bridge is a new execution on previously exposed data, not a new independent confirmation set. Feature-search and total execution costs are not equated with the bridge merely because classifier grids and specimen IDs match.')

S('s4','h1','S4 Feature budgets and selection repeatability')
S('s4a','p','Prescribed-budget analyses use the saved V4 candidate families and retain inner SVM-parameter selection. At K = 62, SNV absorption, joint SNV and raw absorption achieve 81.00%, 87.11% and 88.78%. The complete budget table retains all stored families, not just the three curves in Fig. S1. The primary estimate instead uses K selected inside each outer-training subset. All 930 retained joint-SNV feature occurrences at K = 62 are absorption coordinates; these occurrences are selections, not 930 independent specimens or distinct frequencies.')
S('s4_figure','figure',key='S1')
S('s4_budgets','table',key='budget_core')
S('s4b','p','The mean cross-pipeline K = 62 Jaccard index, 0.0111, compares corresponding joint and absorption-only masks. Within-pipeline stability uses pairs of masks generated by the same method. Primary SNV absorption gives mean K = 220.6, Jaccard 0.8110 and Φ = 0.8360 across 15 outer refits. Its fixed-K (K = 62) sensitivity gives Jaccard 0.6952 and Φ = 0.7976. The candidate universe remains the full 612 coordinates. A joint selector has 1,224 candidates; a conditional absorption-projection summary and a complete-joint-space Φ are different estimands and are not substituted for one another.')
S('s4_stability','table',key='stability')
S('s4c','p','The fixed-training experiment uses the first three complete saved contexts in their frozen order, fold 0 of each; training IDs stay fixed within context. Thirty predetermined Monte Carlo seeds, 2026111600–2026111629, produce two rankings per task. The saved experiment contains 90 paired tasks, 180 rankings and 18,000 PLS fits, with no classifier training. Its plain K = 62 absorption Jaccard is 0.7317–0.7663 and Φ = 0.8253–0.8504. These estimates describe Monte Carlo-seed variation with training specimens fixed, rather than variation in training-subset membership.')
S('s4_mc','table',key='mc_stability')
S('s4d','p','Fixed-gap4 masks are less repeatable at exact coordinates: absorption Jaccard 0.3413–0.3578 and Φ = 0.4236–0.4480 across the three training contexts. These masks arise from the same scores under a spacing constraint; the ranking experiment alone provides no accuracy result. Grouping 4, 8 or 16 grid points is retained as a sensitivity description. Such widths are not established instrument resolution or chemical peak widths. Correlated neighboring coordinates permit substitution, so exact-coordinate agreement and group agreement answer different questions.')
S('s4e','p','In the separate crossed-mask assay, 15 fixed contexts reproduce all original diagonal predictions. Exchanging absorption masks changes mean accuracy by 5.22 percentage points at either available parameter setting, with 13 positive and 2 negative contexts. C = 100 for both arms in every context. Numerical gamma values are equal except for floating-point rounding, and exchanging these parameters changes no class prediction. The parameter factor is therefore not identifiable in those contexts. This is a conditional mask-substitution result at effectively common settings, not proof that hyperparameters are irrelevant, and its distinct split design is not combined numerically with the V4 K = 62 comparison.')
S('s4_cross','table',key='crossed')
S('s4_effect','table',key='paired_effects')

S('s5','h1','S5 Workbook-held-out material and fixed-role source evaluation')
S('s5a','p','LOWO uses all eligible specimens and preserves each complete workbook as a test group once. Fitting uses the remaining workbooks, SNV, a training-fitted scaler, all 612 absorption coordinates and fixed balanced RBF-SVM parameters C = 10 and gamma = scale. It does not use an adaptive MC20 selector. Classifier parameters are not selected on the held-out workbook. All 28 folds, including failed folds, appear below; pooled metrics count every held-out specimen once.')
S('s5_lowo','table',key='lowo_folds')
S('s5b','p','Binary LOWO gives 854/864 correct, 98.84% accuracy, 98.63% balanced accuracy and macro-F1 = 0.9881. Four-material LOWO gives 1,097/1,672 correct, 65.61% accuracy, 63.13% balanced accuracy and macro-F1 = 0.6143. Per-material recalls are Suanzaoren 99.60%, Huangqi 80.65%, Zaoren 45.60% and Tianma 26.67%. Equal-weight workbook mean accuracy is a different estimand and does not replace the pooled score. Fixed-role results use a different population and training composition, so their difference from LOWO is not interpreted as a paired causal effect or as proof of leakage.')
S('s5c','p','Source-task fixed PRED results are Suanzaoren 85/100, Zaoren 72/74, Huangqi 69/82 and Tianma 81/83. The Zaoren majority-source baseline is 70/74, while its balanced accuracy is 60.00% and macro-F1 = 0.5972. Different tasks share specimens and have different class structures. Their results are not independent confirmations or a ranking of task difficulty. Repeatedly developing methods on this collection also leaves historical model-selection exposure; neither repeated partitions nor nested hyperparameter selection retrospectively removes that history.')

S('s5_fixed','p','In the separate fixed-role source-only comparison (sheet T27_fixed_classification), the Suanzaoren five-source task yielded 85.00% accuracy for full_svm (85/100), 87.00% for fixed_MC20 (87/100; macro-F1 = 0.8695), and 94.00% for R0_reselected (94/100). Both selected models use K = 62, 20 Monte Carlo draws and two PLS components. The fixed_MC20 model uses SNV and balanced RBF-SVM C = 10 with gamma = scale; R0 uses raw absorption and C = 100 with gamma = scale, selected by two-fold TRAIN-only historical-grid cross-validation without CAL or PRED selection. The full_svm model uses SNV, all 612 coordinates and C = 10 with gamma = scale. On the four-material fixed-PRED task, full_svm achieved 97.64% (331/339), compared with 92.33% for the 62-coordinate fixed_MC20 model (313/339). These fixed-budget pipelines differ from the primary nested workflow, which selects K internally and retains an average of 220.6 coordinates. The contrasts describe the recorded pipelines and fixed split; they neither isolate a causal feature-budget effect nor establish a general relationship between task breadth and required spectral coverage.')
S('s5_errors','p','Across the three recorded partition repeats of the primary MC20 workflow, the 300 physical specimens produced 900 outer-test predictions and 59 errors. ZY07-to-ZY04 confusions accounted for 30 of these errors and involved 12 distinct specimens; eight showed this confusion in all three repeats. The repeated predictions are correlated observations on the same specimens. This descriptive pattern concerns the recorded source labels and does not establish authenticated identity, duplicate procurement or a geographic or chemical mechanism.')

S('s6','h1','S6 Support-label controls and neural transfer references')
S('s6a','p','The B1–B5 studies retain all corrected positive and negative results. Their 105, 345, 375, 375 and 180 task bundles use overlapping data and partially shared endpoints; they are not five independently acquired cohorts. The five-source controls reported in the main text use the same 300 Suanzaoren TRAIN specimens with 190 Source, 50 Support and 60 Query specimens per context. K-shot 1, 3, 5 and 10 means 5, 15, 25 and 50 total labels. It must not be confused with a separate total-label acquisition budget.')
S('s6_support','table',key='support')
S('s6b','p','Complete refitting at 1, 3, 5 and 10 labels per class achieves 80.00%, 80.67%, 82.11% and 83.89%. At the largest budget, 12 of 15 contexts improve, 2 tie and 1 declines relative to the source model; at 1 or 3 labels per class, 4 contexts decline. Source-scaler/numerical-gamma-frozen refitting gives 83.78%, unlabelled-statistics updating 80.11%, cyclically incorrect-label refitting 73.33%, and correct-label refitting with normalized total loss mass 83.78%. Increasing source-only loss mass remains 80.00%. These observations support a valid-supervision effect within the stated full-spectrum task.')
S('s6c','p','B5 Source-internal strategy selection gives 83.67%, compared with 83.78% for direct frozen-transform refitting. Score motion does not establish useful adaptation: the weak-calibration endpoint changes all 900 score vectors but only 2 predicted classes. Label-acquisition variants retain their actual total budgets and all random controls in the source workbooks; their performance depends on the subsequent learner. These controls do not certify a combined selected-feature/adaptation pipeline that has not been evaluated as a whole.')
S('s6d','p','The fixed-PRED three-seed neural comparison gives 81.00% target-only, 80.33% additional-target-pretraining and 79.00% auxiliary-material-pretraining mean accuracy. The network uses an interpolated 128-point absorption representation and a residual architecture distinct from the nested full 612-point MLP. Complete corrected 1,305 and V4 additional 330 summaries remain in the data workbook, with original protocol and seed definitions. Continuations, backups, repeated predictions and fit counts are not added to the physical specimen count.')

S('s7','h1','S7 Evidence provenance and implementation scope')
S('s7a','p','The data workbook Source_Index and EVIDENCE_MANIFEST.json document the corrected result sources, protocols and file hashes. The 18 original workbooks, specimen-level predictions and selected-coordinate masks provide the basis for the tables and derived interval and stability summaries. The original B1–B5 Excel data are preserved byte for byte.')
S('s7b','p','The later expanded-search worker is separate from the verified V4 and E1 implementations. In its SVM search, the transformation cache reused the first candidate’s numerical gamma, so the declared three-factor search acted as a fixed-first-factor search. Performance and cost from those affected SVM configurations are excluded from the corrected algorithm comparison; this gamma defect does not apply to non-SVM estimators. The crossed-mask assay is interpreted at its executed, effectively common numerical settings.')
S('s7c','p','The paired primary intervals were recomputed consistently from saved same-ID predictions using 10,000 source-stratified physical-ID bootstrap draws, seed 20261116. Each selected physical ID retains all 3 repeated predictions; class counts remain fixed. The resulting intervals are conditional on the saved fits, retrospective and unadjusted for multiple comparisons; they do not include new-batch variability or uncertainty from refitting the full workflow.')
S('s7d','p','The accompanying source workbooks contain the complete corrected alternatives, including low-performing references. Workbook splits and material labels remain separate from source-discrimination splits. Physical specimen IDs and the original TRAIN/CAL/PRED assignments remain available as provenance fields throughout the protocol-specific analyses.')

OPTICAL_EQUATIONS={
 'optical_transfer':r'T(\omega)=\frac{4n(\omega)}{[n(\omega)+1]^2}\exp\!\left[-\frac{\alpha(\omega)d}{2}\right]\exp\!\left[-i\frac{[n(\omega)-1]\omega d}{c}\right]',
 'optical_index':r'n(\omega)=1+\frac{c\Phi(\omega)}{\omega d}',
 'optical_absorption':r'\alpha(\omega)=-\frac{2}{d}\ln\!\left\{|T(\omega)|\frac{[n(\omega)+1]^2}{4n(\omega)}\right\}',
}
